547 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Creating truly inclusive services requires more than good intentions, it demands a conscious, ongoing commitment to understanding and addressing the real experiences of people living in poverty. Poverty-informed services are built on a simple but powerful cycle)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
548 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Asking is the starting point. It means actively reaching out to children, young people, families and communities, especially those who are often underrepresented or excluded, to understand their experiences, challenges and needs. This requires creating safe, accessible opportunities for people to share their views without fear of judgement or stigma.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
549 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Listening goes beyond collecting feedback. It is about valuing lived experience as expertise, recognising patterns in what people tell us and being open to hearing uncomfortable truths. Genuine listening requires reflection, empathy and a willingness to challenge assumptions about how services are designed and delivered.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
550 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
551 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Advocating extends this work beyond individual services. It involves using insight and evidence to influence wider systems, partners and decision-makers, ensuring that the needs of those experiencing poverty are recognised and addressed at every level.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Together, these four elements create a continuous and dynamic approach to service improvement.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
